      "narrative": "The copper lamps hiss softly against the morning rain as you approach Mara Venn. She's leaning against the bar, one hand wrapped around a clay cup, her charcoal coat damp at the shoulders. Her sharp eyes track you as you open your mouth to speak.\n\n\"You're wondering about the Gate,\" she says before you get a word out, her voice flat and unsurprised. \"Everyone who walks in here with that look on their face is.\"\n\nYou ask your question \u2014 what she knows, why it's closed \u2014 and she listens. Her expression doesn't shift, but you notice her thumb tap once against the cup. A patient pause.\n\nThen she speaks, and the words wash over you in a way that leaves you grasping.\n\n\"Right. The Gate.\" She sets her cup down. \"It was sealed by order of the town council, forty years past. The path beyond... it doesn't lead where it used to. Something changed on the other side. Bram used to tell stories\u2014\"\n\nShe stops. Her jaw tightens. \"Bram's dead now. Died at the Gate three nights ago. Throat cut.\"\n\nShe looks at you, waiting for a reaction. You realize, with a dull pulse of frustration, that you've already lost the thread. You were so focused on her *tone*, the way her fingers moved, the crease in her coat \u2014 you were reading *her*, not listening to what she actually said. The details slipped through. You caught the shape of her story but none of the substance: no names, no dates, no *why*.\n\nMara tilts her head. \"You stopped paying attention halfway through, didn't you.\"\n\nIt's not a question."
    },
