      "narrative": "The door of the Copper Finch swings shut behind you, cutting off the hiss of copper lamps and the low murmur of the tavern. The morning air hits you \u2014 damp, cold, carrying the smell of wet stone and distant woodsmoke. Rain falls in a fine, steady curtain, beading on your cloak.\n\nA narrow lane leads away from the tavern's threshold, cobbles slick and dark. The buildings on either side press close, their upper stories leaning inward like eavesdroppers. Shutters are drawn. No one is about.\n\nThe lane bends once, twice, and then opens onto a stretch of bare ground. And there it stands.\n\nThe Ash Gate.\n\nA cracked black arch of stone, perhaps twelve feet high, rising alone against a grey-white sky. No wall flanks it, no structure supports it \u2014 it's just *there*, a freestanding doorframe in an empty field. The stone is pitted and worn, dark as charcoal, and the ground around it is churned mud marked by boot prints, some fresh, some days old. A few dark stains near the base of the arch catch the rain.\n\nThe archway itself is empty \u2014 you can see the grey field and distant treeline through it, uninterrupted. Whatever the Gate once opened onto, it shows nothing now. A file-scarred iron lock hangs from a hasp on the right pillar, its mechanism shattered, the keyhole mangled.\n\nA figure stands near the Gate, hood raised against the rain. Travel-stained cloak, a courier's satchel at their hip. They're staring at the arch, not moving, as if waiting for something."
    },
