      "narrative": "The Copper Finch's door groans open, and warmth spills out to meet you \u2014 the smell of old ale, damp wool, and woodsmoke. The copper lamps still hiss against the rain. A few patrons glance up, then look away. The morning crowd is thin: a man nursing a cup by the fire, a woman reading a letter near the window, Mara Venn behind the bar wiping a glass with slow, deliberate motions.\n\nYour table stands in its usual spot \u2014 scarred oak near the wall, within reach of the hearth's warmth. A half-burned candle stub sits in a pewter holder, wax pooled and cold. Someone's left an empty cup.\n\nMara looks up as you settle in. She sets the glass down, picks up a rag, and walks over. She doesn't ask where you've been.\n\n\"Same as before?\""
